In this lesson we'll look at how angles inside a circle connect to each other. There are three big ideas, and they all link back to the same arc. The first one says that if you draw an arc, the angle made at the very centre of the circle is exactly twice the angle made by that same arc at any point around the outside of the circle, on the larger remaining part of the circle. So centre point, double the size, same arc. The second idea says that anywhere two or more people stand on the circumference looking at the same chord from the same side, they all see the exact same angle. Move them anywhere on that same side of the chord, the angle doesn't change. And the third one is a special, really useful case of the first. If a chord happens to be a full diameter, then the angle on the circumference that looks at it is always a perfect right angle, every single time. The way we prove these is by drawing two radii from the centre to the chord, which gives you an isosceles triangle, and those two equal sides give you equal base angles you can chase through. Now let's see it in action.